<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tegucigalpa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tegucigalpa is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, in a highland valley of the south-centre. It grew from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-mining settlement founded in 1578, and after independence it rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comayagua">Comayagua</a> as the seat of government until it became the permanent capital in 1880. Together with neighbouring Comayagüela it forms the Central District.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa.jpg" alt="Tegucigalpa" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Inforgetmenots · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tegucigalpa_downtown_panoramic_2026.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tegucigalpa lies in the southern-central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>, in a valley ringed by mountains, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a>, of which it is also the departmental capital. The city stands on a chain of mountains, at elevations of 975 metres at its lowest points and 1,463 metres in its highest suburbs, and is hilly with few flat areas. The Choluteca River separates it from its sister city Comayagüela. The surrounding country was open woodland of pine and oak, and the climate is a tropical savanna one, tempered by the altitude, with a rainy and a dry season.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Spaniards claimed the site on 29 September 1578 and founded it as the Real de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> de San Miguel de Tegucigalpa, on the site of an existing settlement of the Lenca and the Tolupans. Juan de la Cueva became its first mayor in 1579. Among its first important buildings were the Dolores Church (1735), the San Miguel Cathedral (1765), the Casa de la Moneda (1780) and the Immaculate Conception Church (1788). The origin of the name is uncertain: the most widely accepted version derives it from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> phrase meaning 'hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>', although the local people are said to have been unaware of the mineral deposits, and other proposals include 'painted rocks'.</p><p>On 30 October 1880 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-aurelio-soto">Marco Aurelio Soto</a> moved the seat of government from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comayagua">Comayagua</a> to Tegucigalpa. The constitution of 1936 made Tegucigalpa and Comayagüela a single Central District, formed on 30 January 1937, and the constitution of 1982 keeps that arrangement as the permanent national capital. In late October and early November 1998 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-mitch">Hurricane Mitch</a> stayed over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> for five days; on saturated ground and deforested slopes the rain caused catastrophic flooding, especially in Tegucigalpa, and destroyed parts of the city along the banks of the Choluteca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tegucigalpa is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and its political and administrative centre, seat of the national government and of the National Autonomous University of Honduras, founded in 1847. Legally the capital is the Municipality of the Central District, though nearly all government institutions stand on the Tegucigalpa side of the river, while Comayagüela on the western bank is largely made up of lower-income neighbourhoods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2013 counted 1,157,509 people in the Central District. Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> has been the leading religion since the Mercedarian missions of about 1548, and the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comayagua">Comayagua</a> was moved to the city and renamed the Diocese of Tegucigalpa by 1916.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tegucigalpa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In colonial times Honduras was a thinly settled province whose silver mines around <b>Tegucigalpa</b> and cattle ranches supported a small creole elite, while its governor and bishop resided at Comayagua.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Costa Rica did not ratify it and Nicaragua withdrew, but Guatemala, El Salvador and Honduras went ahead, and a constituent assembly in <b>Tegucigalpa</b> adopted a federal constitution in September 1921.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-central-america-1921-1922">Federation of Central America (1921-1922)</a></li><li><blockquote>The former province of Comayagua and the mining district of <b>Tegucigalpa</b> formed the State of Honduras within the Federal Republic of Central America.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-honduras">State of Honduras</a></li></ul></section>
